1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
On many bush tracks in south-western Australia, there

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,720
are boot-cleaning stations.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,560
The reason is in the soil: Phytophthora cinnamomi,

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,720
a pathogen that about 40 per cent of

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,240
the region's native plant species are susceptible to.

6
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,640
Yet some banksia species cope with it far

7
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,080
better than others.

8
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:20,080
So how does a plant with no immune

9
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,200
cells defend itself, and why does one banksia

10
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,280
hold out while another dies?

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,940
Phytophthora cinnamomi looks like a fungus, but it's

12
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:32,980
a water mould, a different group with cellulose

13
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:34,020
in its walls.

14
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:37,220
In wet soil it releases swimming spores.

15
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:40,660
They swim towards chemicals leaking from roots, settle

16
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:43,220
just behind the root tip, and grow threads

17
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,980
called hyphae into the root.

18
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:48,260
The threads kill root tissue, so less water

19
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:49,380
reaches the leaves.

20
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:52,740
The plant yellows, wilts and dies back from

21
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:53,620
the roots up.

22
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:56,340
Soil on boots and tyres carries it to

23
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:57,460
new places.

24
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:01,066
A plant can't send defenders to the site,

25
00:01:01,187 --> 00:01:03,946
because it has no circulating immune cells.

26
00:01:04,067 --> 00:01:06,426
So every cell has to defend itself where

27
00:01:06,507 --> 00:01:07,546
it is.

28
00:01:07,627 --> 00:01:11,226
Some defences are always there, before any attack.

29
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:13,626
Others switch on only when a cell detects

30
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:14,506
the pathogen.

31
00:01:15,734 --> 00:01:18,453
The always-there defences are barriers.

32
00:01:19,094 --> 00:01:21,733
As a root ages, its outer cell walls

33
00:01:21,814 --> 00:01:25,093
are toughened with lignin and other waterproof materials,

34
00:01:25,174 --> 00:01:27,333
and older roots and stems grow bark.

35
00:01:27,894 --> 00:01:29,973
A spore's thread has to get past these

36
00:01:30,054 --> 00:01:32,533
walls before it can feed, and it usually

37
00:01:32,614 --> 00:01:34,813
pushes in where two cells meet.

38
00:01:35,094 --> 00:01:37,093
The soft, young part of the root behind

39
00:01:37,134 --> 00:01:40,053
the tip has thin walls and no bark,

40
00:01:40,134 --> 00:01:42,773
so it's the least protected, which is exactly

41
00:01:42,854 --> 00:01:44,133
where the spores settle.

42
00:01:45,334 --> 00:01:47,973
Once a cell detects the pathogen, it starts

43
00:01:48,214 --> 00:01:49,933
building and making chemicals.

44
00:01:50,214 --> 00:01:52,573
It lays down callose, a plug made of

45
00:01:52,614 --> 00:01:54,693
a sugar chain, on the inside of its

46
00:01:54,854 --> 00:01:57,253
wall right where a thread is pushing in.

47
00:01:57,734 --> 00:02:00,133
It adds more lignin to nearby walls.

48
00:02:00,374 --> 00:02:04,693
And it makes antimicrobial chemicals, such as phytoalexins,

49
00:02:04,774 --> 00:02:07,013
which poison or slow the pathogen.

50
00:02:08,234 --> 00:02:11,513
The boldest response is the hypersensitive response.

51
00:02:12,074 --> 00:02:15,273
Cells at and around the infection kill themselves,

52
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:17,193
fast, on purpose.

53
00:02:17,834 --> 00:02:20,713
Phytophthora feeds on living cells at first, so

54
00:02:20,794 --> 00:02:22,793
a ring of dead cells leaves it nothing

55
00:02:22,874 --> 00:02:24,553
to feed on and fences it in.

56
00:02:25,194 --> 00:02:27,353
A signal can also travel through the plant

57
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:28,953
and prepare distant tissue.

58
00:02:29,514 --> 00:02:31,993
But each response only helps if it arrives

59
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:32,873
in time.

60
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:37,220
So what happens in real banksias, and does

61
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:38,340
timing matter?

62
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:42,660
In a 1998 study, researchers infected banksia seedlings

63
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:45,660
with Phytophthora's swimming spores and watched under a

64
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:46,740
microscope.

65
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:50,340
In scarlet banksia, from Western Australia, root tips

66
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:52,580
were dead within a day, and the damage

67
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:55,060
reached the seedling's stem by day five.

68
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:57,420
Callose was laid down late.

69
00:02:57,780 --> 00:02:59,700
In old man banksia, from the New South

70
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,820
Wales coast, root damage only started on day

71
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:05,780
three, and by day nine it rarely reached

72
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:06,980
halfway up the root.

73
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,200
Both species laid down callose.

74
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:12,680
The clearest difference is timing.

75
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,040
In old man banksia, something held the damage

76
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:17,000
to the lower root.

77
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:20,680
Scarlet banksia showed rapid root damage and later

78
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:24,120
callose deposition, but these observations do not show

79
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:26,280
that the hyphae outran the defence.

80
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,040
And in a related species, hairpin banksia, the

81
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:32,280
threads took longer even to get in.

82
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:34,860
Notice the limits, though.

83
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:37,980
The microscope work used seedlings a few weeks

84
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:40,620
old, in a lab, in one study.

85
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:44,860
Tolerant isn't immune: old man banksia still lost

86
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:45,820
part of its root.

87
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:48,700
Adult plants in the field can behave differently.

88
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:52,380
And this pathogen probably arrived from overseas, so

89
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:55,100
neither banksia evolved alongside it for long.

90
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:57,900
Yet old man banksia held it back.

91
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:00,700
A short history with a pathogen doesn't mean

92
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:01,660
no defence.

93
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:04,620
A species relies on the defences it already

94
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:05,340
has.

95
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:07,926
That shapes control.

96
00:04:08,247 --> 00:04:11,846
Hygiene stops the spread: cleaning boots and tyres,

97
00:04:11,887 --> 00:04:13,606
and closing tracks in wet weather.

98
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:16,886
For threatened plants, phosphite can be sprayed or

99
00:04:16,967 --> 00:04:17,606
injected.

100
00:04:18,087 --> 00:04:20,566
It doesn't remove the pathogen from the soil,

101
00:04:20,647 --> 00:04:23,206
but treated plants wall it off faster, which

102
00:04:23,287 --> 00:04:25,846
helps the plant's own defences win the race.

103
00:04:27,067 --> 00:04:31,146
So remember: the hypersensitive response is a firebreak.

104
00:04:31,707 --> 00:04:34,266
Dead cells around the infection leave a pathogen

105
00:04:34,347 --> 00:04:36,586
that needs living cells with nothing to feed

106
00:04:36,667 --> 00:04:40,186
on, and speed decides whether the break holds.

107
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:42,840
Two exam questions now.

108
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:43,800
First.

109
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:47,480
Roots of two plant species, X and Y,

110
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:49,960
were infected with Phytophthora cinnamomi.

111
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:53,320
In species X, callose was laid down at

112
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,960
entry sites within 6 hours, and the dead

113
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:57,880
zone stopped at 8 millimetres.

114
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:02,200
In species Y, callose appeared after 2 days,

115
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:04,920
and the dead zone reached 45 millimetres in

116
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:05,800
a week.

117
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:09,560
Identify the more resistant species, and explain how

118
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,640
its response limited the pathogen.

119
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:13,560
Pause here to answer.

120
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:26,206
Species X is more resistant.

121
00:05:26,607 --> 00:05:28,846
Its callose plugged the walls where the threads

122
00:05:28,927 --> 00:05:31,646
were pushing in within hours, so the pathogen

123
00:05:31,727 --> 00:05:34,046
was held near the entry sites, and only

124
00:05:34,127 --> 00:05:35,886
8 millimetres of root died.

125
00:05:36,447 --> 00:05:39,406
In Y, the plug came after two days,

126
00:05:39,487 --> 00:05:42,446
too late, so the pathogen spread much further.

127
00:05:43,167 --> 00:05:46,126
Choosing Y because more of its tissue died,

128
00:05:46,207 --> 00:05:48,126
as if that were a bigger defence, is

129
00:05:48,207 --> 00:05:49,566
the classic mistake.

130
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:51,326
Second.

131
00:05:51,807 --> 00:05:53,886
A leaf is infected by a fungus that

132
00:05:53,967 --> 00:05:56,566
can feed only on living cells.

133
00:05:56,847 --> 00:05:59,646
Within hours, the cells around the infection site

134
00:05:59,807 --> 00:06:02,366
die, leaving a small brown spot.

135
00:06:03,007 --> 00:06:05,406
Over the next three weeks the spot stays

136
00:06:05,487 --> 00:06:07,326
the same size, and the rest of the

137
00:06:07,407 --> 00:06:08,526
leaf stays green.

138
00:06:09,247 --> 00:06:11,886
A student says, the dead spot shows the

139
00:06:12,007 --> 00:06:13,926
plant's defences have failed.

140
00:06:14,207 --> 00:06:15,806
Evaluate the student's claim.

141
00:06:16,287 --> 00:06:17,646
Pause here to answer.

142
00:06:28,067 --> 00:06:29,906
The claim isn't supported.

143
00:06:30,147 --> 00:06:33,106
Cells around the infection died within hours, and

144
00:06:33,187 --> 00:06:34,626
the spot stopped growing.

145
00:06:35,107 --> 00:06:38,626
That fits the hypersensitive response working: this fungus

146
00:06:38,787 --> 00:06:41,506
needs living cells, so the dead ring fences

147
00:06:41,587 --> 00:06:43,106
it in, and the rest of the leaf

148
00:06:43,187 --> 00:06:44,226
stays healthy.

149
00:06:44,547 --> 00:06:46,946
If the brown area kept spreading, that would

150
00:06:47,147 --> 00:06:48,706
suggest the defence was failing.

151
00:06:49,347 --> 00:06:51,746
Reading every dead cell as the plant losing

152
00:06:51,907 --> 00:06:53,506
is the classic mistake.

153
00:06:54,734 --> 00:06:57,693
So for any named plant and pathogen, say

154
00:06:57,774 --> 00:07:00,573
how the pathogen gets in, which defences meet

155
00:07:00,654 --> 00:07:03,453
it, always there or switched on, and what

156
00:07:03,534 --> 00:07:05,933
the evidence shows about timing.

157
00:07:06,174 --> 00:07:08,813
Next, what happens first when a pathogen gets

158
00:07:08,894 --> 00:07:09,853
into an animal.

159
00:07:11,067 --> 00:07:12,506
Here are the key notes.

160
00:07:12,747 --> 00:07:14,666
Pause the video if you need more time

161
